1311:   "text": "Contrary to the reading that return and capture are one value in `look_path`: at src/observe.rs:314-330 `printed` is a distinct `String::new()` that receives a COPY of `shown` via `printed.push_str(&shown)` (a borrow), and only afterwards is `shown` MOVED into `PendingEntry { output: shown, .. }`. Between the push_str and the move there are two independent strings, so a bound applied to `printed` at that point cannot reach `entry.output`, the entry's `label`, its `key`, its `world_root`/`world_state`, or anything that ships into the snapshot. Whatever `look_path` captures is byte-identical whether or not `printed` was bounded. The `label` computed at 297-312 is likewise built from `contents`/`lines`, never from `printed`.",
1409:   "text": "Two things about refusing rather than reporting a bound in `look_grep`.

(1) The ordering does NOT force report-over-refuse. In the match branch (src/observe.rs:438-478) `printed.push_str(&stdout)` and the exclusion note run FIRST, before `by_file` is built and before any `PendingEntry` is constructed; the `Search` entry and the per-file `GrepMatch` entries are pushed onto the in-memory `buf` after that, and `pending::save(workspace_dir, &buf)?` is the last statement before `Ok(LookOutcome { printed })`. So an implementation that bounds while assembling `printed` reaches the bound before any entry exists and before anything is persisted, and could return `Err` there discarding nothing. Nothing in this range makes a refusal impossible.

(2) What does decide it is the crate's own report-versus-refuse discriminator, stated in `targets.rs`'s module doc (targets.rs:44-49): the census refusal is a refusal because "Here nothing is left to tell: the remedy is singular. Run the workspace-rooted grep, mint the fact, cite it — or withdraw the target", as against `scope`, which "stayed human-owed because only a person can tell context from conclusion". A whole-`src` grep for `refuse` shows every `workspace::refuse` call site in the crate is of that singular-remedy kind — a missing or malformed input, or a forbidden flag combination: claims.rs (no --proposition, no such claim, --revise without --why, unknown cited fact), facts.rs (empty note, no such fact), prose.rs (--ack combined with any of --text/--revise/--heading/--level/--cites/--before, --level out of 1..=6), targets.rs (no symbol, uncensused symbol), transplants.rs (empty --text, withdrawn ids, an unanswered premise at render), observe.rs (no such path, invalid --lines range, `--grep` without a path, `lines` with `grep`). Not one refusal anywhere in src is keyed to the size or content of a result. A byte budget is not a singular-remedy condition: the author who hits it has no act that re-runs the search under the budget.",
1503:   "text": "What `look_path` selects and what it wraps that selection in, read at src/observe.rs:277-337 for the purpose of settling the budget's referent on the path side. (1) The selected text is `shown`: with no `--lines` it is `contents.clone()`, the whole file; with `--lines a:b` it is `all[a - 1..end].join("\n")` over `contents.lines()`, where `end = b.min(total)` — so the selection is 1-based, begins at line `a`, and is joined without a trailing newline. (2) `printed` is a separate `String::new()` assembled in up to three pushes: `printed.push_str(&format!("==> {path} <==\n"))`, then `printed.push_str(&shown)`, then a single `'\n'` if `shown` is non-empty and does not already end in one. (3) The header therefore exists in `printed` and in no other place: `PendingEntry { output: shown, .. }` receives the selection alone, so neither the header nor the trailing-newline pad is ever part of the captured output, the label, the key or anything that ships in the snapshot. A budget measured over the assembled `printed` would consume the header before any content, and would count `==> ` plus the path plus ` <==\n` against the file's own bytes; a budget measured over `shown` counts only what was selected.",
