1573:   "text": "`run` has this ticket's defect and this design does not fix it — read so that the standing non-coverage entry can be written honestly about what it does and does not cover. (1) `run_command` (src/observe.rs:537-621) captures the child's merged stdout/stderr into one buffer, converts it with `String::from_utf8_lossy`, and then uses the same value twice: `output: output_text.clone()` into a `PendingEntry` of kind `ObservationKind::Proc` keyed by the command line and labelled `proc: {cmdline} (exit {exit_code})`, and `Ok(RunOutcome { printed: output_text, .. })` as the return. Nothing anywhere in the function measures, bounds or truncates either copy, and the label carries the command line and the exit code and no measure of size. (2) Both front ends hand that return back whole: the CLI does `print!("{}", outcome.printed)` (src/main.rs:526) and the MCP handler returns it as the `"output"` field of a structured result (src/mcp.rs:652-657), neither measuring it. (3) The `run` tool's own description (mcp.rs:649) already contemplates the large case and answers it by warning the author rather than by bounding anything: "The whole output is captured verbatim and, once minted into a fact, becomes permanently unrevisable and ships into the snapshot beside the memo — so a command whose output is enormous, or contains a credential, puts that in a repository forever." So a `run` return overruns the caller's cap exactly as an unbounded `look` return does, and the only thing standing against it today is that warning. Note the census scope here: this fact opens `run_command`, its two call sites and the `run` tool description; it is not a whole-worktree census of any symbol.",
1616:   "text": "In `look_path` the label is finished before the string the bound applies to exists — the opposite statement order from `look_grep`. `let (shown, label) = match lines { ... };` completes at observe.rs:297-312 and fixes `label` in all three of its arms (`path.to_string()`, the "nothing in range" form, and `{path} lines {a}-{end}`), and only afterwards, at observe.rs:314, does `let mut printed = String::new()` begin assembling the return. The label is then moved into `PendingEntry { label, .. }` at 321-330 unchanged. So an implementer who bounds `printed` at 316 where `shown` is pushed, and leaves the `match lines` block untouched, gets a cut return with a label that says nothing about the cut, which is the silent cut the design exists to stop. In `look_grep` the same edit is naturally safe: `printed.push_str(&stdout)` at observe.rs:438 opens the match branch and the `Search` label is built afterwards at 457-460.",

497:   "text": "src/observe.rs lines 196-258, the tail of `look_grep` — the return path and the retention path, side by side, with no cap on either.

Return path: on a matching search, `printed.push_str(&stdout)` copies the grep's entire stdout into `printed`, and `printed` is what `LookOutcome` carries back to the caller at the end of the function. There is no length check, no truncation, no elision, and no configuration anywhere between the grep's stdout and that return.

Retention path: separately, the same stdout is split by file and each file's matching lines are pushed into a `PendingEntry` whose `output` is `matches.join("\n")` — also uncapped. The whole-search `Search` entry carries `output: String::new()` and a `label` of the form "search: {root} (grep: {pattern}) — {files} file(s) matched"; the zero-match `NoMatch` entry likewise carries empty output and a "no-match: {pattern} in {root}" label. So the whole-search record is the one entry in a grep's fold that has a label and no output of its own.

Nothing in this function bounds what it returns, and nothing in it records that anything downstream did.",
