(2) What does decide it is the crate's own report-versus-refuse discriminator, stated in `targets.rs`'s module doc (targets.rs:44-49): the census refusal is a refusal because "Here nothing is left to tell: the remedy is singular. Run the workspace-rooted grep, mint the fact, cite it — or withdraw the target", as against `scope`, which "stayed human-owed because only a person can tell context from conclusion". A whole-`src` grep for `refuse` shows every `workspace::refuse` call site in the crate is of that singular-remedy kind — a missing or malformed input, or a forbidden flag combination: claims.rs (no --proposition, no such claim, --revise without --why, unknown cited fact), facts.rs (empty note, no such fact), prose.rs (--ack combined with any of --text/--revise/--heading/--level/--cites/--before, --level out of 1..=6), targets.rs (no symbol, uncensused symbol), transplants.rs (empty --text, withdrawn ids, an unanswered premise at render), observe.rs (no such path, invalid --lines range, `--grep` without a path, `lines` with `grep`). Not one refusal anywhere in src is keyed to the size or content of a result. A byte budget is not a singular-remedy condition: the author who hits it has no act that re-runs the search under the budget.",
1385:   "text": "What `look_path` selects and what it wraps that selection in, read at src/observe.rs:277-337 for the purpose of settling the budget's referent on the path side. (1) The selected text is `shown`: with no `--lines` it is `contents.clone()`, the whole file; with `--lines a:b` it is `all[a - 1..end].join("\n")` over `contents.lines()`, where `end = b.min(total)` — so the selection is 1-based, begins at line `a`, and is joined without a trailing newline. (2) `printed` is a separate `String::new()` assembled in up to three pushes: `printed.push_str(&format!("==> {path} <==\n"))`, then `printed.push_str(&shown)`, then a single `'\n'` if `shown` is non-empty and does not already end in one. (3) The header therefore exists in `printed` and in no other place: `PendingEntry { output: shown, .. }` receives the selection alone, so neither the header nor the trailing-newline pad is ever part of the captured output, the label, the key or anything that ships in the snapshot. A budget measured over the assembled `printed` would consume the header before any content, and would count `==> ` plus the path plus ` <==\n` against the file's own bytes; a budget measured over `shown` counts only what was selected.",
1426:   "text": "`run` has this ticket's defect and this design does not fix it — read so that the standing non-coverage entry can be written honestly about what it does and does not cover. (1) `run_command` (src/observe.rs:537-621) captures the child's merged stdout/stderr into one buffer, converts it with `String::from_utf8_lossy`, and then uses the same value twice: `output: output_text.clone()` into a `PendingEntry` of kind `ObservationKind::Proc` keyed by the command line and labelled `proc: {cmdline} (exit {exit_code})`, and `Ok(RunOutcome { printed: output_text, .. })` as the return. Nothing anywhere in the function measures, bounds or truncates either copy, and the label carries the command line and the exit code and no measure of size. (2) Both front ends hand that return back whole: the CLI does `print!("{}", outcome.printed)` (src/main.rs:526) and the MCP handler returns it as the `"output"` field of a structured result (src/mcp.rs:652-657), neither measuring it. (3) The `run` tool's own description (mcp.rs:649) already contemplates the large case and answers it by warning the author rather than by bounding anything: "The whole output is captured verbatim and, once minted into a fact, becomes permanently unrevisable and ships into the snapshot beside the memo — so a command whose output is enormous, or contains a credential, puts that in a repository forever." So a `run` return overruns the caller's cap exactly as an unbounded `look` return does, and the only thing standing against it today is that warning. Note the census scope here: this fact opens `run_command`, its two call sites and the `run` tool description; it is not a whole-worktree census of any symbol.",
1450:   "text": "In `look_path` the label is finished before the string the bound applies to exists — the opposite statement order from `look_grep`. `let (shown, label) = match lines { ... };` completes at observe.rs:297-312 and fixes `label` in all three of its arms (`path.to_string()`, the "nothing in range" form, and `{path} lines {a}-{end}`), and only afterwards, at observe.rs:314, does `let mut printed = String::new()` begin assembling the return. The label is then moved into `PendingEntry { label, .. }` at 321-330 unchanged. So an implementer who bounds `printed` at 316 where `shown` is pushed, and leaves the `match lines` block untouched, gets a cut return with a label that says nothing about the cut, which is the silent cut the design exists to stop. In `look_grep` the same edit is naturally safe: `printed.push_str(&stdout)` at observe.rs:438 opens the match branch and the `Search` label is built afterwards at 457-460.",

446:   "text": "src/observe.rs lines 196-258, the tail of `look_grep` — the return path and the retention path, side by side, with no cap on either.

