20:{"event":"Create","id":"C15","prop":"A census enumerates call sites, not the modes those sites select, and this is a distinct printed limit rather than the insincere-examination ceiling restated. Distinct because the two limits bound different things: the ceiling bounds interpretation — capture makes the extent honest, not the reading of it — while the mode limit bounds the artifact itself. What look_grep captures per matched file is the matching lines only; an argument that selects materially different behaviour (a None versus a Some, a flag, a mode enum) is sometimes visible on a match line, never enumerated, and absent entirely when the value arrives through a variable, a default, or another line of a multi-line call. So even a sincerely read census does not, in general, contain which mechanism each caller engages — the reader must leave the census and read the callee and each call's context, which is why collapsing this into \"whether anyone read the census\" would misstate the remedy. It does not change the design: surfacing argument values at census time would require parsing call expressions, which is per-language knowledge — exactly the coupling the project's language-agnostic-by-construction doctrine exists to exclude — and it would not change the trigger either, since a value-surfacing census would still need the same declared-target trigger. It costs one more line in the census's non-coverage: the census gets an implementer to the door of every textual call site; it does not say what happens inside any of them.","from":["F2","F9","F5"],"timestamp":1786133277}

